# How many of the layers each relation actually reaches. One row per relation: the layers it
# touches, against every layer there is.
#
# A layer a relation does not reach is outside it
#   The measurement is the boundary itself. The three relations a process notation would have drawn
#   as arrows reach almost nothing: `draw`, `induction` and `coupling` touch a handful of
#   layers each, `part` reaches more, and `nameplate` and `slack` reach every layer that states
#   them, which is nearly all; a layer with no stated nameplate is outside them, and
#   `queries/observations/18-layer-reach.sqlc` names it. So the links between layers are sparse in
#   this model and the figures are nearly everywhere, and BPMN can carry only the links.
#   `diagrams/domain_objects.sqlc` says which tables render as an element and which are refused with
#   a typed reason; this says how much each one holds.
#
# Read the other way, it is `rank/layer_reach.sqlc`
#   This relation counts, per relation, how many layers it touches, so it can say how many and never
#   which, and a layer nothing reaches leaves no row here to be counted. Read the other way, the
#   subject is the layer and the shortfall has a name. That relation depends on
#   `checks/grain.sqlc`'s `unresolvable` arm: a rule returning a claim address in the contract's
#   `layer` column makes a count taken through that key measure the key.
#
# Why a diagram of this corpus looks empty and is not wrong
#   The largest tables here are `absence`, `claim`, `narrowing` and `bound_origin`, one row per
#   filed blank or value, and not one of them has a BPMN element. A reader who concludes "there is
#   nothing here" has read a faithful rendering of the part a notation can hold.
#
# The base is a column here
#   Not in the observation that reads it. `reaches` alone is a bare count, and a count says nothing
#   about coverage until something says of how many; the `--` line claims the comparison, so the
#   relation owes it, rather than each caller adding it. `guard_reach` carries `subjects` beside
#   `witnessed`, and `layer_reach` is driven from every layer, so without this column this would be
#   the one relation of the three a reader had to supply the other half of.
#
# Every layer, in the one role that passes nothing on
#   `algebra/dimension_use.sqlc` allows a base that drives the query or is left-joined, and forbids
#   an inner join, because every consumer would inherit the whole of it. Read as a single number it
#   joins to no row here at all, so nothing downstream can inherit a bound from it.
#
# The numbers
#   Run this relation for them; the corpus moves and a sentence does not.
-- every relation of either kind, counted against layers/every_layer.sqlc as the base.
SELECT x.relation, x.kind, x.reaches,
       (SELECT count(*) FROM ( :compose(layers/every_layer.sqlc) ) l) AS of_layers
FROM      (
    SELECT 'draw'        AS relation, 'incidence' AS kind,
           count(DISTINCT (d.filing, d.layer)) AS reaches
    FROM ( :compose(entries/draws.sqlc) ) d
    UNION ALL
    SELECT 'induction',   'incidence', count(DISTINCT (n.filing, n.layer))
    FROM ( :compose(entries/inductions.sqlc) ) n
    UNION ALL
    SELECT 'coupling',    'incidence', count(DISTINCT (c.filing, c.from_layer))
    FROM ( :compose(entries/couplings.sqlc) ) c
    UNION ALL
    SELECT 'part',        'incidence', count(DISTINCT (p.composition, p.composed_layer))
    FROM ( :compose(composition/parts.sqlc) ) p
    UNION ALL
    SELECT 'nameplate',   'magnitude', count(DISTINCT (n.filing, n.layer))
    FROM ( :compose(layers/nameplate.sqlc) ) n
    UNION ALL
    SELECT 'slack',       'magnitude', count(DISTINCT (s.filing, s.layer))
    FROM ( :compose(entries/slacks.sqlc) ) s
) x
